//! crypto/pbkdf2_sha512.rye — Season G (Cryptography), a key-derivation rung: PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA-512 (RFC 8018 / RFC 2898) authored in pure
//! Rye. PBKDF2 is the standard "stretch a password into a key by iterating a keyed hash many times" primitive; run with SHA-512 as its PRF
//! it is the exact stretch BIP-39 folds a mnemonic and passphrase into a 512-bit wallet seed with (2048 rounds, salt "mnemonic"‖passphrase),
//! and the KDF several LUKS and PGP deployments run. It authors no new cryptography: it composes the GREEN crypto/hmac_sha512.rye through
//! RFC 8018's own iteration-and-XOR, so it is buildable this round and provable against an independent implementation and the HMAC beneath.
//! It stands beside crypto/pbkdf2_sha256.rye (the same construction over a 32-byte PRF) and crypto/argon2.rye (memory-hard, where this is
//! iteration-hard) — a security audit expects the tree to hold each, and to say which each caller should reach for.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season G thread (Cryptography · Rye-native, Monocypher-parity, audit-ready).
//! Design read: active-designing/20260815-175524_rye-first-crypto-parity-and-the-decision-wave.md (Season G · the crypto parity wave).
//! Kin: crypto/hmac_sha512.rye (the keyed hash beneath it, GREEN on metal) · crypto/sha512.rye (the hash beneath that) ·
//! crypto/hkdf_sha512.rye (a sibling that composes the same HMAC, extract-and-expand rather than iterate-and-XOR) ·
//! crypto/pbkdf2_sha256.rye (the SHA-256 sibling of this exact construction). The coming BIP-39 mnemonic→seed rung stands on this one.
//! Monocypher (vendor/monocypher, CC0/BSD-dual) is the parity DISCIPLINE target — our own Rye, studied clean-room from the public
//! standards (.claude/rules/gratitude-licenses.md).
//!
//! THE CRUX — RFC 8018 §5.2, PRF = HMAC-SHA-512, nothing more:
//!   DK = T(1) ‖ T(2) ‖ … ‖ T(l), truncated to dkLen, where l = ceil(dkLen / hLen) and each block
//!   T(i) = U(1) XOR U(2) XOR … XOR U(c), with U(1) = HMAC(P, S ‖ INT_32_BE(i)) and U(j) = HMAC(P, U(j-1)).
//! The password P keys every HMAC; the salt S with the big-endian block index seeds the first U; the count c is the whole cost — c HMAC
//! calls per block make each password guess c times as expensive. No new mathematics, only the RFC's composition over a MAC a lower rung
//! already proved. The same password, salt, iteration count, and length always fold to the same key.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL, TEST inputs, no real identity key, no network, no funds, no real device. The module calls
//! no std.crypto in the primitive. Parity is proven at selftest the same no-fabricated-constant way crypto/secp256k1_pubkey.rye favours —
//! against Zig's own std.crypto.pwhash.pbkdf2 with HmacSha512 as an INDEPENDENT implementation, byte-for-byte across several shapes
//! including a multi-block output and a partial tail; AND transitively against the RFC-4231-anchored HMAC beneath it — a single-iteration
//! block is exactly HMAC(P, S ‖ INT_32_BE(1)) over the already-GREEN crypto/hmac_sha512.rye. The world-bound published known-answer for
//! this exact PRF is BIP-39's own Trezor vector; it arrives with the BIP-39 rung this one unblocks. Constant-time discipline is the HMAC
//! layer's named horizon; PBKDF2 derives a key and returns it.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run crypto/pbkdf2_sha512.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const sha512 = @import("sha512.rye");
const hmac = @import("hmac_sha512.rye");

pub const pbkdf2_sha512_version = "20260816.073849";

/// PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA-512's fixed block width, inherited from the HMAC beneath so a single source of truth names it: each T(i) block is one
/// 64-byte HMAC-SHA-512 output, and the derived key is a run of these blocks truncated to the requested length.
pub const dk_block_len: u32 = hmac.tag_len;

/// The largest inputs this primitive accepts, each refused at the edge before a byte is mixed. The password keys every HMAC, so it sits
/// within HMAC's own key bound; the salt is followed by a 4-byte block index inside the first HMAC message, so it sits four bytes below
/// HMAC's message bound. Real uses — a passphrase, a per-user salt, a BIP-39 "mnemonic"‖passphrase salt — sit far below these, yet the
/// bounds are named so every buffer cites a ceiling and each HMAC call stays provably within crypto/hmac_sha512.rye's own bounds.
pub const max_password_bytes: u32 = hmac.max_key_bytes;
pub const max_salt_bytes: u32 = hmac.max_message_bytes - 4;

/// The iteration-count ceiling. RFC 8018 allows an enormous count; this names a practical bound far above any real deployment (BIP-39 runs
/// 2048, modern password stores hundreds of thousands) so the loop that iterates the PRF cites a maximum rather than trusting a caller.
pub const max_iterations: u32 = 1 << 24;

/// The largest derived key one call yields. PBKDF2's theoretical ceiling is (2^32 − 1)·hLen; this names a practical bound so the block
/// index stays far below 2^32 and the output buffer cites a maximum. Real derived keys — a 64-byte BIP-39 seed, a 32-byte session key —
/// sit far below 4096 bytes.
pub const max_dk_bytes: u32 = 1 << 12;

/// The five faults this primitive can raise, each refused at the edge before a byte is mixed.
pub const PbkdfError = error{
    /// A password longer than max_password_bytes — refused before it keys the first HMAC.
    PasswordTooLong,
    /// A salt longer than max_salt_bytes — refused before the first HMAC message is filled.
    SaltTooLong,
    /// A zero iteration count — RFC 8018 requires c ≥ 1; refused before any block is computed.
    IterationsZero,
    /// An iteration count above max_iterations — refused before the PRF loop begins.
    IterationsTooMany,
    /// A requested key longer than max_dk_bytes — refused before a block is filled.
    OutputTooLong,
};

/// RFC 8018 §5.2 block function F(P, S, c, i) = U(1) XOR U(2) XOR … XOR U(c), where U(1) = HMAC(P, S ‖ INT_32_BE(i)) and each later
/// U(j) = HMAC(P, U(j-1)). Returns the one 64-byte block; the caller places it into the derived key. Bounds are the public pbkdf2's job,
/// asserted here as preconditions, so every HMAC call is provably within bounds and its error is unreachable rather than swallowed.
fn block(password: []const u8, salt: []const u8, iterations: u32, index: u32) [dk_block_len]u8 {
    // invariant: RFC 8018 requires at least one iteration — the caller refuses a zero count before reaching here.
    assert(iterations >= 1);
    // invariant: the block index is a positive INT_32_BE value, 1..=l, bounded far below 2^32 by max_dk_bytes.
    assert(index >= 1);
    // invariant: the password fits HMAC's key bound, so it keys every HMAC below without faulting.
    assert(password.len <= max_password_bytes);
    // invariant: the salt fits four bytes below HMAC's message bound, so salt ‖ INT_32_BE stays within it.
    assert(salt.len <= max_salt_bytes);

    // The first HMAC's message is the salt with the big-endian block index appended: S ‖ INT_32_BE(i).
    var msg: [max_salt_bytes + 4]u8 = undefined;
    const slen: u32 = @intCast(salt.len);
    // invariant: the salt-length cast lost nothing — it is bounded by max_salt_bytes above.
    assert(slen <= max_salt_bytes);

    var p: u32 = 0;
    var k: u32 = 0;
    while (k < slen) : (k += 1) {
        msg[p] = salt[k];
        p += 1;
    }
    msg[p] = @as(u8, @truncate(index >> 24));
    p += 1;
    msg[p] = @as(u8, @truncate(index >> 16));
    p += 1;
    msg[p] = @as(u8, @truncate(index >> 8));
    p += 1;
    msg[p] = @as(u8, @truncate(index));
    p += 1;

    // U(1) = HMAC(P, S ‖ INT_32_BE(i)); the message is at most max_salt_bytes + 4 = HMAC's message bound and the password is within
    // HMAC's key bound, so this mac cannot fault — asserted, not swallowed.
    var u = hmac.mac(msg[0..p], password) catch unreachable;
    // The running XOR accumulator that becomes F(P, S, c, i); it begins as U(1).
    var acc = u;

    var j: u32 = 2;
    while (j <= iterations) : (j += 1) {
        // U(j) = HMAC(P, U(j-1)); the message is a 64-byte block, well within HMAC's bound, so this mac cannot fault either.
        u = hmac.mac(&u, password) catch unreachable;
        var b: u32 = 0;
        while (b < dk_block_len) : (b += 1) acc[b] ^= u[b];
    }
    return acc;
}

/// PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA-512 — fill dk with the derived key stretched from password and salt over `iterations` rounds. The whole "expensive by
/// design" arc: dk is T(1) ‖ T(2) ‖ … truncated to dk.len, each block c HMAC calls deep.
pub fn pbkdf2(dk: []u8, password: []const u8, salt: []const u8, iterations: u32) PbkdfError!void {
    // invariant: the password fits HMAC's key bound, so it keys every HMAC below.
    if (password.len > max_password_bytes) return error.PasswordTooLong;
    // invariant: the salt fits four bytes below HMAC's message bound, so salt ‖ INT_32_BE stays within it.
    if (salt.len > max_salt_bytes) return error.SaltTooLong;
    // invariant: RFC 8018 requires c ≥ 1 — a zero count would derive no work and is refused.
    if (iterations == 0) return error.IterationsZero;
    // invariant: the iteration count fits the named ceiling, so the PRF loop is bounded.
    if (iterations > max_iterations) return error.IterationsTooMany;
    // invariant: the request fits the named ceiling, so the block index stays far below 2^32.
    if (dk.len > max_dk_bytes) return error.OutputTooLong;

    const total: u32 = @intCast(dk.len);
    // invariant: the output-length cast lost nothing — it is bounded by max_dk_bytes above.
    assert(total == dk.len);

    var done: u32 = 0;
    var index: u32 = 1;
    while (done < total) : (index += 1) {
        // invariant: the block index stays a positive INT_32_BE value; max_dk_bytes keeps it far below 2^32.
        assert(index >= 1 and index <= (max_dk_bytes / dk_block_len) + 1);

        const t = block(password, salt, iterations, index);

        // Copy this block into the key: a whole 64-byte block, or the final partial tail that finishes the request.
        const remain: u32 = total - done;
        const take: u32 = if (remain < dk_block_len) remain else dk_block_len;
        var c: u32 = 0;
        while (c < take) : (c += 1) dk[done + c] = t[c];
        done += take;
    }
    // invariant: the loop fills exactly the requested length, no more and no less.
    assert(done == total);
}

/// Independent reference: Zig's own std.crypto.pwhash.pbkdf2 with HmacSha512, used ONLY inside the selftest as a second implementation to
/// prove our authored composition matches byte-for-byte. The primitive above never calls this.
fn ref_pbkdf2(dk: []u8, password: []const u8, salt: []const u8, iterations: u32) void {
    const HmacSha512 = std.crypto.auth.hmac.sha2.HmacSha512;
    // The selftest passes only valid parameters (c ≥ 1, dk within bound), so the reference cannot raise its weak-parameters error.
    std.crypto.pwhash.pbkdf2(dk, password, salt, iterations, HmacSha512) catch unreachable;
}

fn block_eq(a: [dk_block_len]u8, b: [dk_block_len]u8) bool {
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < dk_block_len) : (i += 1) {
        if (a[i] != b[i]) return false;
    }
    return true;
}

fn slice_eq(a: []const u8, b: []const u8) bool {
    if (a.len != b.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < a.len) : (i += 1) {
        if (a[i] != b[i]) return false;
    }
    return true;
}

/// Prove our authored PBKDF2 equals Zig's independent std.crypto.pwhash.pbkdf2 for one (password, salt, iterations, length) shape,
/// byte-for-byte.
fn ref_parity(password: []const u8, salt: []const u8, iterations: u32, out: []u8, ref_out: []u8) !void {
    // invariant: the two output buffers are the same length, so a byte-for-byte compare is meaningful.
    assert(out.len == ref_out.len);
    try pbkdf2(out, password, salt, iterations);
    ref_pbkdf2(ref_out, password, salt, iterations);
    if (!slice_eq(out, ref_out)) return error.ParityMismatch;
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // 1. Independent-implementation parity, byte-for-byte against Zig's std.crypto.pwhash.pbkdf2 with HmacSha512 — a second implementation
    //    as the oracle, no fabricated constant trusted, exactly the way crypto/secp256k1_pubkey.rye proves itself. Several shapes: a single
    //    iteration; the BIP-39 depth (2048 rounds, "mnemonic" salt) folding to a 64-byte seed; a multi-block key spanning three 64-byte
    //    blocks with a partial third; and a short partial tail that does not land on a block boundary.
    {
        // Shape A — single iteration, 64-byte key.
        {
            var out: [64]u8 = undefined;
            var ref: [64]u8 = undefined;
            try ref_parity("passwd", "salt", 1, &out, &ref);
        }
        // Shape B — the BIP-39 iteration depth and salt prefix, 64-byte seed: 2048 rounds over "mnemonic"‖passphrase, the exact shape the
        //    coming BIP-39 rung folds a mnemonic and passphrase into a wallet seed with.
        {
            var out: [64]u8 = undefined;
            var ref: [64]u8 = undefined;
            try ref_parity("legal winner thank year wave sausage worth", "mnemonicTREZOR", 2048, &out, &ref);
        }
        // Shape C — a multi-block key: 150 bytes over 4096 iterations spans three 64-byte blocks with a partial third.
        {
            var out: [150]u8 = undefined;
            var ref: [150]u8 = undefined;
            try ref_parity("multi block password", "multi block salt", 4096, &out, &ref);
        }
        // Shape D — a short partial tail that does not fill a block boundary, to prove the truncation lands.
        {
            var out: [37]u8 = undefined;
            var ref: [37]u8 = undefined;
            try ref_parity("tail", "tail salt", 1000, &out, &ref);
        }
    }

    // 2. A single-iteration block ties transitively back to the RFC 4231-anchored HMAC: PBKDF2 with c = 1 makes the first block exactly
    //    HMAC(P, S ‖ INT_32_BE(1)), reproduced against the already-GREEN crypto/hmac_sha512.rye.
    {
        const password = "tie to the hmac beneath";
        const salt = "a salt";
        var dk: [dk_block_len]u8 = undefined;
        try pbkdf2(&dk, password, salt, 1);

        // The first HMAC message is the salt with INT_32_BE(1) appended — "a salt" is 6 bytes, so 10 bytes with the index.
        var built: [10]u8 = undefined;
        var b: u32 = 0;
        while (b < salt.len) : (b += 1) built[b] = salt[b];
        built[b] = 0;
        built[b + 1] = 0;
        built[b + 2] = 0;
        built[b + 3] = 1;
        const via_hmac = try hmac.mac(built[0 .. salt.len + 4], password);
        if (!block_eq(dk, via_hmac)) return error.SingleIterNotHmac;
    }

    // 3. The bounds refuse over-long inputs, a zero and an over-large iteration count, and an over-long request before a byte is mixed.
    {
        var small: [8]u8 = undefined;

        const oversize_password: []const u8 = @as([*]const u8, @ptrFromInt(0x1000))[0 .. max_password_bytes + 1];
        if (pbkdf2(&small, oversize_password, "s", 1)) |_| return error.OversizePasswordSlipped else |err| if (err != error.PasswordTooLong) return error.WrongOversizePassword;

        const oversize_salt: []const u8 = @as([*]const u8, @ptrFromInt(0x1000))[0 .. max_salt_bytes + 1];
        if (pbkdf2(&small, "p", oversize_salt, 1)) |_| return error.OversizeSaltSlipped else |err| if (err != error.SaltTooLong) return error.WrongOversizeSalt;

        if (pbkdf2(&small, "p", "s", 0)) |_| return error.ZeroIterSlipped else |err| if (err != error.IterationsZero) return error.WrongZeroIter;

        if (pbkdf2(&small, "p", "s", max_iterations + 1)) |_| return error.TooManyIterSlipped else |err| if (err != error.IterationsTooMany) return error.WrongTooManyIter;

        const oversize_out: []u8 = @as([*]u8, @ptrFromInt(0x1000))[0 .. max_dk_bytes + 1];
        if (pbkdf2(oversize_out, "p", "s", 1)) |_| return error.OversizeOutSlipped else |err| if (err != error.OutputTooLong) return error.WrongOversizeOut;
    }

    print("GREEN crypto-pbkdf2-sha512: PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA-512 authored in pure Rye — Season G's iteration-hard password KDF (RFC 8018) with " ++
        "SHA-512 as its PRF, the exact stretch BIP-39 folds a mnemonic and passphrase into a 512-bit wallet seed with (2048 rounds, salt " ++
        "\"mnemonic\"‖passphrase), and the KDF several LUKS and PGP deployments run. It composes the GREEN crypto/hmac_sha512.rye through " ++
        "the RFC's own iterate-and-XOR: each block T(i) = U(1) XOR … XOR U(c) with U(1) = HMAC(P, S ‖ INT_32_BE(i)) and U(j) = HMAC(P, " ++
        "U(j-1)), the derived key the run of 64-byte blocks truncated to length — no new cryptography, only the RFC's composition over a " ++
        "MAC a lower rung already proved. It stands beside crypto/pbkdf2_sha256.rye (the SHA-256 sibling) and crypto/argon2.rye (memory-" ++
        "hard, where this is iteration-hard), and the coming BIP-39 mnemonic→seed rung stands on it. Parity is proven the no-fabricated-" ++
        "constant way crypto/secp256k1_pubkey.rye favours: byte-for-byte against Zig's independent std.crypto.pwhash.pbkdf2 with HmacSha512 " ++
        "across single-iteration, the BIP-39 2048-round depth, multi-block, and partial-tail shapes; AND transitively against the RFC-4231-" ++
        "anchored HMAC beneath — a single-iteration block is exactly HMAC(P, S ‖ INT_32_BE(1)). The world-bound published known-answer for " ++
        "this PRF is BIP-39's own Trezor vector, arriving with the BIP-39 rung this one unblocks. An over-long password, salt, or request, " ++
        "a zero count, and an over-large count each refuse a named error before a byte is mixed. Purely local — TEST inputs, no real " ++
        "identity key, no network, no funds, no real device; constant-time discipline stays the HMAC layer's named horizon.\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
